Herein, Lx $\scriptstyle(\mathbf{x}=1-4)$ have very similar structure and coordination chemistry as the parent linker, i.e. btc3-, and only one carboxylate ligator site is replaced by weaker interacting ligators. This systematic doping with defective linkers can not only offer the opportunity to finely tune the engineered defects on CUS but also to modify the MOFs’ porosity due to defects clustering and the coordination modulation by defective linkers during synthesis and crystal growth.38 In the parent, "perfect" form, $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(b t c)_{2}]}$ exhibits two ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites as the native CUS at the axial positions of the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CR})_{4}$ paddlewheel nodes of the framework. We obtained the series of DEMOFs Dx of formula $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(b t c)_{2-d}(L x)_{d}]}$ $\scriptstyle(\mathbf{x}=1-4)$ listed in Table S1 of
3.2. Probing Local defects by UHV-FTIR Spectroscopy and DFT Modelling.
Representative samples were selected for characterization of CUS by CO adsorption and in situ UHV-FTIRS at $90~\mathrm{K}$ . The corresponding IR spectra for different DEMOF samples prepared by varying the defective linkers Lx (L1-L4) with comparable doping levels $(15\%-20\%)$ ) or by increasing the concentration of L4 (pydc ), are presented in Fig. 2 and Fig. 3, respectively. The expected bands between 2179 and $2174~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ stem from CO molecules bound to native ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ CUS through electrostatic and ${\mathfrak{o}}$ -donation interactions similar to the parent $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}b t c_{2}]}$ .41 However, the samples D1-D4 exhibit an intense and broad second $\mathrm{C-O}$ stretching region ranging from 2130 to $2100~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , which turned out to be highly sensitive to the nature (Fig. 2) and doping level of Lx (Fig. 3). The pronounced red-shifted IR bands suggest a variety of CO species adsorbed at electronically modified, i.e. reduced copper sites, due to the enhanced $\pi$ back-donation.48 The assumption of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{1+}$ formation (defective $\mathrm{Cu}^{1+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ pairs) is consistent with the absence of anions A, and is confirmed by XPS results (see Fig. S11-15 in SI for details). In addition, the generation of reduced $\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$ CUS by defective linker incorporation agrees with the shift of the band gap to higher energies (diffuse reflectance spectra, Fig. 8) and the results of the magnetic measurements (Fig. 9). The framework is redox responsive, and thus it compensates for the missing carboxylate by ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{1\dagger}$ formation.49-50 A quantitative analysis of the integrated intensity ratio of the $\bar{\mathrm{Cu}}^{+}$ -related to the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -related CO bands (Fig. 3d) reveals that the abundance of $\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\mathrm{CUS}$ in D4 is gradually increased with increasing the degree of L4 incorporation. It should be noted that the extinction coefficient of ${\mathrm{CO-Cu}}^{+}$ is generally more than one order of magnitude higher than that of CO-Cu2+,48,51 indicating a very low abundance of $\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$ (native) defects in the parent HKUST-1 (few $\%$ ).
In order to corroborate this reasoning and to gain deeper insight into the nature of the generated defects at the copper paddlewheel moieties, QM/MM calculations of isolated defects were performed. The key strategy of the calculations is that a sufficiently large quantum mechanical (QM) part (dispersion corrected hybrid DFT level) is mechanically embedded into a (periodic) molecular mechanics (MM) model using the QM derived MOF-FF force field45 to maintain the defec
