tive linkers in the proper structure. Full details about the theoretical methods and results are given in the SI. The calculations focused on D4 with the largest deviation from the parent structure and D1 with the smallest deviation as inferred from the UHV-IR experiments (Fig. 2-3). Different binding modes of CO at the CUS have been considered, but only for $\eta^{1}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}$ coordinated CO stable minima could be optimized, for which vibrational normal modes (for the QM subsystem) have been computed in turn (Fig. 4 and S17). First of all, an assignment of the experimentally observed CO bands to a non-reduced $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ defect can safely be ruled out by the calculations, corroborating the experimental results. On the other hand, the matching of the calculations with the UHV-IR spectra is in favor of the reduced $\mathrm{Cu}^{1+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ defect. Importantly, a theoretical estimate of the relative redox potentials (using a continuum solvation model) indicates, that the cationic $\mathrm{Cu}^{\top+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ defect is much more prone to reduction as compared to the native $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ paddle wheel (see SI). The additional unpaired electron delocalizes over both copper sites with a symmetric charge distribution for the CO-free defective $\mathrm{Cu}^{1+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ paddlewheel unit (Table S6). This is supported by the XPS data which show that the $\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{1\it{+}}}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions in the $\dot{\mathrm{Cu}}^{1+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ dimer cannot be distinguished (Fig. S12).
Upon coordination of the first CO (Fig. 4a), the additional electron is localized to generate a ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{1+}$ site binding one CO with strong $\pi$ back-donation. The calculated frequency of 2097 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ together with the relatively high binding energy of 28.1 $\operatorname{kcal}/\mathrm{mol}$ for the $(\mathrm{CO)Cu^{1+}}$ species generally explains the redshifted CO bands around $21\dot{2}0~\mathrm{cm}^{-\bar{1}}$ of the spectra (Fig. 2-3). Coordination of the second CO at the remaining ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ site (Fig. 4b) exhibits a lower binding energy of $9.6\ \mathrm{kcal/mol}$ with the corresponding $\mathsf{v}(\mathrm{CO})$ mode $(2179\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ ) falling in the same range of non-defect $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ paddlewheels, which are also present in the samples. The alternative, simultaneous coordination of the first and the second CO at the same ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{1+}$ site (Fig. 4c) retaining the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ site vacant is ${\sim}6.5\ \mathrm{kcal/mol}$ less stable, consequently, such a situation is unlikely in the real samples. Most significantly, the calculated results indicate that a third CO molecule may weakly bind $(2\mathrm{\kcal/mol})$ to the electron rich ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{1+}$ site, if the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2\dag}$ site is already occupied (Fig. 4d). The frequency of the resulting asymmetric CO stretch mode of this geminal $\mathrm{{(CO)_{2}C u}^{1+}}$ species calculated to $2123~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ corresponds to the characteristic feature around $2150~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in the UHV-IR spectra. Due to the weak binding, it will be the least populated and is the first thermally removed (see Fig. 5-6 and the discussion below). This third CO coordination results from the more open coordination space at the defective CUS. Such species could not be found for the parent $[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{btc}_{2}]$ by our DFT calculations.41 Interestingly, a similar stable minimum could not be located in case of D1 as the steric situation of L1 (5- nitro-benzene-1,3-dicarboxylate, nidc2-) is much closer to btc3- than L4 (pydc2-, see Table S6). This is in accord with the experimental observation that the feature around $2150~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for D1 is nearly invisible (Fig. 2b).
The data derived from modelling of D1 and D4 are used to assign the $\upnu(\mathrm{CO})$ bands for D2 and D3. Noticeably, the molecular models considered in this study could not explain the blue shifted band around $2200~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ in the experimental spectra. It may be related to minority CO bound to other types of defect sites probably located at the external crystallite surface or at cracks inside the crystallites.41 These theoretical results are used to extrapolate to the real systems, which is particularly valid for lower framework incorporated Lx concentration $\mathrm{\hat{d}=}$ $\sim5\%$ ), e.g. D4a (Fig. 3a). For higher concentrations $(\mathrm{d}>20\%)$ the influence of nearest neighbor defects is likely to become more important leading to an increasing diversity of environments for different species, and thus the spectroscopic signatures are more complicated (e.g. D4c, Fig. 3c).
Based on this theoretical analysis, we have a closer inspection of the experimental data set of Fig. 2-3 obtained by probing CUS of D1-D4 with CO adsorption, starting out again from D4 (Fig. 3). Upon increasing concentration of L4 (pydc ), the dominating CO bands located at ${\sim}2179\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ assigned to ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -CO species (either parent or defective sites) become more and more asymmetrical, wider and gradually red shifted, before splitting into two bands peaked at 2177 and $2169~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Increasing the CO equilibrium pressure makes these changes more visible. Accordingly, the band at $2127~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ with a shoulder band becomes wider and red shifted. Subsequently, the shoulder evolves into a sharp peak at $2115~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ superimposed by a new shoulder band at ${\sim}2101~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . High incorporated concentrations $\mathrm{\ddot{d}}>20\mathrm{\%}$ ) of L4 result in a quite complex pattern with red-shifted and superimposed bands (Fig. 3c). These band evolutions upon stepwise increasing incorporation of L4 prove that the defect degree, i.e. mixed valence $\mathrm{Cu}^{1+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{\dot{2}+}$ formation can be precisely controlled by the choice of Lx and doping level d. Interestingly, D1-D3 exhibit similar but less drastic effects depending on the nature of L1-L3 and the applied doping level (Fig. 2b-d). For example, the lowest intensity bands ranging from 2150 to $2157~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ of D1-D4 (Fig. 2-3), assigned to the weakly bound second ${\mathrm{CO-Cu}}^{1+}$ species, is indicative for the enhanced coordination space at the defect (Fig 4c; Fig. S16, S17). Such band is almost absent in D1e (Fig 2b) although it has significant doping concentration $(16\%)$ . This observation is clearly related to the ligator properties of the nitro group with the largest steric bulk among Lx and closest similarity to the missing carboxylate ligator of btc3-. Finally, notice the case of D3. The pronounced bands at 2125 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $2105~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ assigned to $\mathrm{C\hat{u}}^{\hat{1}+}$ (Fig. 2d) illustrate that this system prefers copper reduction over deprotonation of the 5-hydroxy group for charge compensation in the course of L3 incorporation.
Temperature-dependent experiments of CO desorption on different DEMOFs (D1-D4) monitored by UHV-FTIRS can provide solid spectroscopic evidence for the binding strength of guest molecules at CUS, which is important for the catalytic behavior of materials (Fig. 5: varying the defective linkers Lx; Fig. 6: increasing the doping level d of L4). In all cases, with increasing sample temperatures, the bands ranging from 2200 to $2160~\mathrm{\bar{cm}^{-1}}$ assigned to $(\mathrm{CO})\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , and the bands at 2160- $2150~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ being characteristic for one CO additionally binding to a $\mathrm{(CO)Cu^{1+}}$ site, show a rapid decrease in intensity with relatively low binding energies (CO desorption at 100-130 K). The CO bands at lower frequencies $(2150{\cdot}2100~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ ) originating from $\mathrm{(CO)Cu^{1+}}$ species are more stable and disappear only upon heating to $200{-}230\ \mathrm{K}$ , revealing that CO is more strongly bound to reduced ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{1+}$ CUS with a higher binding energy due to enhanced $\pi$ back-donation. Notably, the stability of the $(\mathrm{CO})\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ species in DEMOFs is comparable or only slightly lower than that of the $(\mathrm{CO})\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ in the parent $[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{btc}_{2}]$ .41 These general features of the modified CUS of DEMOFs match very well with our QM/MM results of CO binding energies (Table S6). Interestingly, D3e exhibits a very special behaviour that the band at $2157~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (assigned to the asymmetric stretch mode of $(\mathrm{CO})_{2}\mathrm{Cu}^{1+},$ appears significantly more stable compared to all the other samples. A probable reason for this inconsistency is that CO molecules at the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{1+}$ site may form a weakly stabilizing hydrogen bond with the proximal 5- hydroxy group of L3 (hydc2-, see Scheme 1), which is not possible for the other Lx (Fig. S10). Sample D3e shows that DEMOFs with electronically modified CUS and simultaneously functionalized proximal coordination space can be derived. Such local tailoring of CUS may be very useful in multistep or cascade catalytic reactions.52
The derived atomistic model of diluted defects indicates control of the adsorbate stability at the modified CUS of DEMOFs. The stabilities of the $(\dot{\mathrm{CO}})\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ for D4a-c tend to decrease along with increasing doping level of L4 (Fig. 6), illuminating that the introduced local defects may have effects on the whole framework. Notably, the corresponding behaviour of the $\mathrm{(CO)_{a}C u^{\mathrm{1+}}}$ species $(\mathsf{a}=1,2)$ is particular sensitive to Lx. Overall, these close inspections of the UHV-FTIR data show band evolutions and stability differences of CO adsorbed at $\mathrm{Cu}$ sites upon either doping the different kinds of Lx or increasing the degree of Lx incorporation, indicating that the particular effects on the relative abundance and stability of the specific $\mathrm{(CO)_{a}C u^{n+}}$ species can be considered as a function of Lx and the doping level.
## 3.3. $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption Properties
Standard $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption-desorption experiments were carried out for D1-D4 with various Lx concentrations (Fig. 7). The obtained isotherms confirm permanent porosity, framework rigidity, and different types of introduced mesopores. To get deeper understanding of these results, it should be correlated with the modification of CUS as discussed above. As qualitatively expected from the structure of L1 with $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ ligator of steric bulk comparable to carboxylate (Scheme 1), the isotherms of D1c (Fig. 7A, trace a) reveal no formation of mesopores. In contrast, linkers L2-L4 are more effective in modifying CUS locally and creating large-scale defects. Accordingly, D2b (Fig. 7B, trace a), and D3c (Fig. 7C, trace b) exhibit pronounced Type-H4 hysteresis loop isotherms.53 D4b (Fig. 7D, trace a) shows Type-H2 hysteresis loop, and D4c (Fig. 7D, trace b) with further increased concentration of L4 exhibits an even more pronounced hysteresis loop with a step-wise desorption isotherm.54 These findings reveal the formation of mesopores with different structures in D2-D4. The pore size distribution curves of both D3 and D4 determined by the Barret-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) method show the modulated and hierarchical porosity upon varying the defective linkers and their framework incorporated concentrations (Fig. 7C-D).54 All samples, except D4c, exhibit high values for the Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface areas, being comparable to parent $[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{btc}_{2}]$ (Table S5). 55 The observation of a slight decrease of the BET surface area values for the DEMOFs D1-D4 obtained by doping with defective linkers can be attributed to the increase of pore sizes, resulting in the formation of mesopores.38 It is well known that the the presence of mesopores is typically accompanied by certain loss of specific surface areas for the modified MOFs.38-39,46-47,56-57 In addition, the corresponding IR and TGA data in SI show that all the dominating features are characteristic for HKUST-1 frameworks, indicating no significant unreacted ligands remaining in their pores after being washed and activated. Therefore, we conclude that the hysteresis loops of isotherms observed for the DEMOFs result from the mesopore formation with different pore structures. The origin of mesopore formation for all DEMOFs may be ascribed to the different type of local vacancy associated with the molecular structure and the different coordination modulation properties of the defective linkers Lx during the MOF synthesis.38 The coordination chemistry of Lx (coordination bond equilibria) affects nucleation and crystal growth different from the parent linker btc3-.58 Upon raising the concentration of the introduced Lx, the associated, slightly different kind of coordination vacancies at the (defective) copper paddle wheel units may cluster. This effect eventually breaks the three types of neighboring pores of the parent structure (Fig. S8), and may connect each other to generate the observed large mesopores. However, the involved mechanisms are certainly very delicate as the isotherms of D1e and D3e are exhibiting Type-H2 hysteresis loops which are distinct from those of the corresponding reference samples synthesized from $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ rather than $\operatorname{Cu}({\mathrm{BF}}_{4})_{2}$ . While notably, parent $[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{btc}_{2}]$ derived from $_\mathrm{H}\mathrm{_3btc}$ with either of the two salts $\mathrm{CuA}_{2}$ show no mesopores at all. These observations illuminate template effects during synthesis, which are attributed to cooperative interactions between counter anions A (i.e. $\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{BF}_{4}^{-}$ ) of the employed $\mathrm{CuA}_{2}$ and Lx. Consequently, the small-scale and large-scale defects are closely correlated, and can be controlled by the choice of Lx, doping level d and template anion A.
3.4. Evaluation of Reactive Properties.
The parent $[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{btc}_{2}]$ is highly active for CO oxidation under clean UHV conditions at temperatures as low as $105\mathrm{~K~}$ .41 Accordingly, the most defect rich samples D4 were tested for CO oxidation under the same conditions (Fig. S20). The IR data revealed that high levels of mixed valence $\mathrm{Cu}^{1+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ sites present in D4 exhibit special reactive properties due to the electronic modification at the CUS. The ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites at $\mathrm{Cu}^{1+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ defects remain highly active for CO oxidation which occurs at $93~\mathrm{K}$ , while CO is more strongly bound to the reduced ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{1+}$ CUS and does not react with $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ forming $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at low temperatures. Currently, we are investigating more complex reactions such as alcohol oxidation at DEMOFs. It was found that the parent $[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{btc}_{2}]$ is inactive for methanol oxidation, whereas this reaction occurs at the CUS of D4 due to the coexistence of $\mathrm{Cu}^{1+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ dimers and the functional defective linker L4 (pydc2-). A detailed account on the high reactivity of DEMOFs for methanol oxidation will be published elsewhere. Furthermore, we are studying the analogous [Ru3btc2] case59-60 and discovered that the same kind of defect engineering modulates the electronic structure of the framework $\mathtt{R u}$ sites very significantly. The obtained multifunctional DEMOFs $(\mathrm{Ru})$ show quite unusual reactivity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction in UHV at 90 K in the dark (Fig. S21), and are active catalysts for selective hydrogenation of olefins and for tandem reactions (cyclisation/hydrogenation).
3.5. Optical and Magnetic Properties.
The systematic defective linker doping offers fine tuning of the DEMOFs’ band gap. Fig. 6a compares the diffuse reflectance spectra of perfect HKUST-1 with D1-D4. The reflection peaks of the DEMOFs exhibit a wider range and a stronger intensity. The band gap of as-synthesized (hydrated) parent HKUST-1 is determined to be about $2.87~\mathrm{eV}$ , while the assynthesized DEMOFs have larger band gaps ranging from 3.31 to $3.87\mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 8b). Accordingly, the visible color of the as-synthesized DEMOFs is paler than that of the parent material, showing that the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{1+}$ sites are already present in the assynthesized samples. From these data it is concluded that the $\dot{\mathrm{Cu}}^{1+}$ species are predominantly formed during synthesis (ethanol as reducing agent) and are not significantly generated by the activation protocol (heating under vacuum).
Accordingly, the magnetism of the mixed-valence DEMOFs is responding to the degree of defective linkers doping and the corresponding modulation of the CUS. The data obtained for parent HKUST-1 and D4b and D4c are discussed in the following. In general, all $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ vs. $T$ plots (Fig. 9) show a continuous decrease upon cooling. This behavior is characteristic of antiferromagnetic exchange coupling for the parent paddle wheel units of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ which are more or less abundant within all the samples. Noticeably, the $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ vs $T$ plots deviate from each other due to the different structural changes, affecting the electron spin resonance, magnetic coupling constant $J$ of the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ dimmers and additional spin exchanges $(Z J^{\prime})$ via btc and L4, respectively. The cw X-band EPR spectra at room temperature (Fig. S9) of all three samples show a broad signal with an isotropic $g$ value about 2.14 and different line widths ranging from $820\mathrm{G}$ to $1060\mathrm{~G~}$ . This signal is typical for the $S=1$ excited spin state of the antiferromagnetically coupled parent dimers of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ that are involved in additional spin exchange interactions via the aromatic framework.61 The defective $\mathrm{Cu}^{1+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ dimers can be considered as monomeric, open-shell ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ species $\mathrm{3d}^{9}.$ , $S=1/2$ , while closed shell ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{1+}$ is diamagnetic, no coupling). Therefore, based on the measured $g$ values, the magnetic susceptibility data can be fitted using a refined model. It is based on the one described by O. Kahn for such
## Journal of the American Chemical Society
samples containing ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ paddle wheel units together with (diluted) monomeric ${\mathrm{\bar{C}u}}^{2+}$ species,62 and takes the additional spin exchange via btc3- and $\mathsf{\Gamma}\dot{\mathrm{L}}4~(\mathsf{p y d c}^{2-})$ into consideration. In the Supporting Information, the used model and fitting procedure is described in detail. The model fits well with $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ vs $T$ plots for all the samples (Fig. 9; best fitting parameters and agreement factors $R$ are listed in Table S7). The increasing number of defects and fraction of diamagnetic impurity, assigned to $\mathrm{Cu}^{1+}$ , are reflected in the growing values of the fitting parameters $\uprho$ (uncoupled copper centers) and $\sigma$ copper centers coupling via L4; see Table S7) and the lower $g$ value compared to the experimental results. The sum of $\rho$ and $\sigma$ is more or less larger than that deduced from the compositional data (elemental analysis and HPLC). The number of copper centers that do not couple over the $\mu$ -acetato bridge is not directly related to the number of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{1+}$ in the sample. The $J$ values of D4b and D4c of $-330.2{\pm}4.0\$ and $-262.1{\pm}9.\bar{3}~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively, are lower than that of the parent sample $-365.9{\pm}1.7~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (which value is comparable to previously reported data). The comparison of the $J$ values indicates that the spin exchange interactions in the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ dimers decrease along with the increased framework incorporated concentration (d) of L4. For the parent one, the value of spin exchange interactions via the aromatic framework $(Z J^{\prime})$ is $\hat{7}0.1{\pm}2.2~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , while that for D4b and D4c are $-63.5{\pm}2.7\$ and $-41.8{\pm}2.6~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively. These characteristic differences are attributed to the different spin exchange interactions between the dimers, which are regulated by the doping linker as well as its framework incorporated concentration and spatial distribution. These results all together further confirm the assumption of specific $\mathrm{Cu}^{1+}$ formation (already during synthesis, while not during activation and/or thermal treatment), which is controlled by the chosen doping linker Lx.
## 4. CONCLUSIONS
Our experimental results combined with theoretical calculations demonstrate consistently that MOFs can be surprisingly tolerant for systematic incorporation of defective linkers with broad functional variations of modified ligators to generate libraries of single-phased materials isoreticular to the parent structure. Defect engineering of MOFs will simultaneously modify the electronic properties and the proximate coordination space at reactive framework metal centres (i. e. CUS), and as well the pore structure of the MOF, eventually giving rise to functionalized mesopores in the case of stronger defect generating properties and higher concentrations of implemented fragmented linkers. This multivariate nature of DEMOF represents a new dimension for tailoring functions, which holds promise for moving beyond adsorption, separation and catalysis by targeting also physical properties such as band gap engineering (Fig. 8), magnetism (Fig. 9) and eventually conductive properties.63
